# FLYING OVER THE ISLAND (owner, 2026-09-18: "a view where I can zoom out over
# the entire world and see everything, and travel over everything and zoom into a
# given landscape so I can explore the world and give meaningful feedback").
#
#   tools/tour.sh tours/flyover.tour --seed=4 --hour=11 --weather=clear:0 --dev=view
#
# WHAT THE FRAMES HAVE TO SHOW, in order, because a flyover that is only a wider
# camera proves nothing: the ground on the player's shoulder, the same ground from
# the air with the island reading as an island, a different landscape reached by
# flying rather than by warping, and the player still standing where he was left.
#
# The last is the one worth failing on. This is a camera and not a cheat — the
# body does not move, the world is not paused — so what he is being shown is the
# game as it really is, which is the whole reason to be able to look at it.

coast calm
tap dev_toggle
await app:none 5
wait 1.0
shot 01-on-his-shoulder

# --- up ------------------------------------------------------------------------
tap dev_fly
await flying 4
wait 1.5
shot 02-off-the-ground with land:coast

# Out, four steps, each a fifth again: the island stops being a clearing and
# starts being a coastline.
press dev_fly_out 0.15
press dev_fly_out 0.15
press dev_fly_out 0.15
press dev_fly_out 0.15
wait 2.5
shot 03-the-island-reads-as-an-island

# ALL THE WAY OUT, which has to hold the WHOLE world and nothing but it. At 45
# degrees of yaw a square world is a diamond on the glass, so the frame must
# carry its diagonal, and the pitch foreshortens the down axis -- both of which
# the old ceiling ignored, so the far end of the zoom left the world short of the
# frame with black under it and the focus still pinned wherever the player stood
# (owner, 2026-09-18: "the bottom 3rd or so is always solid black space and the
# world isnt centred").
# HELD, not tapped: each step is a factor of 1.22, so the ceiling is some
# seventeen of them away from where the flyover opens and four taps is nowhere
# near it. This is the one frame that can catch the fault at all.
press dev_fly_out 6.0
wait 1.0
shot 03b-the-whole-world-centred

# AND AGAIN, WITHOUT BUILDING IT A SECOND TIME (owner, 2026-09-18: "when I zoom
# out and the world finally loads, I zoom in, and then zoom out again and it has
# to reload"). All the way back down to walking height and all the way out
# again, then held for barely longer than a frame: if anything in the view is
# rebuilt on the way, this frame is caught with it half missing. The coarse
# world is built once and never dropped, and the chunks that leave the square
# are parked rather than freed, so the only honest evidence is the picture taken
# before there would have been time to make one.
press dev_fly_in 6.0
wait 0.5
press dev_fly_out 6.0
wait 0.15
shot 03c-the-same-world-again-with-nothing-rebuilt
same 03b-the-whole-world-centred 03c-the-same-world-again-with-nothing-rebuilt 3

# --- across --------------------------------------------------------------------
# Flying, not warping: the ground streams under the picture the whole way, which
# is the half `at NAME` can never show.
walk 0,-1 4.0
wait 2.0
shot 04-crossed-by-flying-not-by-warping

# --- and down into a place -----------------------------------------------------
press dev_fly_in 0.15
press dev_fly_in 0.15
press dev_fly_in 0.15
wait 2.0
shot 05-down-into-what-is-actually-there

# --- back where he was ---------------------------------------------------------
tap dev_fly
await grounded 4
wait 1.5
shot 06-the-body-never-moved

# --- every region picked out and named ------------------------------------------
# The instrument the owner asked for: which run of a landscape is which, where it
# gives way to the next, and its name on it. F6, in the air or on the ground.
tap dev_fly
await flying 4
press dev_fly_out 0.15
press dev_fly_out 0.15
press dev_fly_out 0.15
press dev_fly_out 0.15
wait 2.0
tap dev_regions
wait 1.2
shot 07-every-region-named
